Day#13 - Scene Understanding & Spatial Mapping

Introduction--
--Scene understanding in Virtual Reality (and Mixed Reality) is the computer's ability to scan, map, and label physical real-world rooms (walls, floors, furniture)
so digital objects can realistically interact with physical spaces. Key functions include surface mapping, semantic labeling, and collision detection.

How it works--
--Scanning: Cameras and sensors on the headset map the shape of your room.
--Labeling: Artificial intelligence gives names to items, identifying a floor, wall, desk, or couch.
--Interaction: Virtual items can bounce off your real walls or digital characters can sit on your physical sofa.

Advantages--
--Realism: Virtual objects hide behind real furniture (occlusion) instead of floating strangely.
--Safety: Virtual characters or tools navigate around your actual room layout safely without hitting real obstacles.
--Mixed Reality: Blurs the line between the real world and digital content seamlessly.

Real World Example--
1)Apple Vision Pro

--Apple Vision Pro uses scene understanding and spatial mapping to recognize walls, floors, tables, windows, and furniture.
--This allows virtual apps and objects to remain anchored in the correct positions within the user's environment.

2)Meta Quest 3

--Meta Quest 3 scans the user's room to create a 3D spatial map.
--Mixed reality games and applications use this map to place virtual objects on real surfaces and avoid obstacles in the room.

